# Shell and text tooling

## Measure the tool, do not reason about it

Every pattern below was measured, not recalled, and several behave differently
from what the manual implies. A shell one-liner that "obviously" does X needs one
run against a known input before you build on it, because the failure mode is
usually **silent success**.

```sh
# never assume. Run both directions: must match, must not match.
printf 'a\r\nb\r\n' > /tmp/crlf.txt
printf 'a\nb\n'     > /tmp/lf.txt
LC_ALL=C grep -qU $'\r' /tmp/crlf.txt && echo found    # yes
LC_ALL=C grep -qU $'\r' /tmp/lf.txt   && echo found    # no
```

## The pattern that never matches

```sh
grep -q $'\r' file        # never matches anything
grep -qP '\r' file       # never matches anything, -P does not help
```

GNU grep consumes a trailing carriage return in the pattern as part of the
pattern's own line terminator. What remains is an empty pattern, and **an empty
pattern matches nothing** in this position — the check reports success on a file
full of the exact defect it exists to find.

`-U` is the fix: it drops newline sensitivity, so the CR is an ordinary byte
mid-line.

## The pattern that matches everything

The opposite failure, and more dangerous, because it looks like success until it
reports a crisis.

```sh
grep -q $'\r\n' file       # matches every file, always
```

A **newline inside a grep pattern splits it into multiple patterns**, and the
trailing remainder is empty, and an empty pattern matches any line. So a
CRLF detector built this way reports the entire repository as defective, the
obvious response is to normalise everything, and after that nothing looks wrong.

Same class, different tool:

| Construct | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `grep -q "$unset" f` | matches everything |
| `grep -q 'a\|\|b' f` | matches everything |
| `grep -q '^$' f` | matches every blank line, not every line |
| `sed "s/$v/x/"` with `v` empty | inserts `x` everywhere |
| `find "$dir" -name "$g"` with `g` empty | non-empty result you did not expect |

**Test the empty input explicitly.** It is the input that decides whether a check
is safe.

## Sed intervals have a floor

```sh
sed 's/^#\{1,\} \{0,\}//' file     # error: Unmatched \{ or "Unmatched \{"
```

`\{0,\}` is not a valid interval: the minimum repetition count may not be zero.
GNU sed reports the error and **exits non-zero**, but a script that discards the
status prints nothing and looks like it worked. Use a simpler form instead:

```sh
sed 's/^#*  *//' file
```

## Names the shell already owns

Assigning to these is silently ignored or silently wrong — no error, no warning:

| Name | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `GROUPS` | a bash builtin array; assignment is discarded |
| `close` | an awk built-in function; used as a variable it yields no output |
| `UID`, `EUID`, `PPID`, `SECONDS`, `RANDOM`, `LINENO` | set by the shell |
| `IFS`, `PATH`, `HOME`, `PWD`, `OSTYPE`, `HOSTNAME`, `OPTARG` | the shell owns them |

This fails as **empty output**, not as a crash, so the symptom appears far from
the cause: a generated file comes out empty and the generator looks innocent.

## Bash cannot hold a NUL

A command substitution strips NUL bytes, so "searching for NUL with grep" cannot
be expressed as a string operation. Compare byte counts instead:

```sh
is_text() {
  raw="$(head -c 8192 "$1" | wc -c)"
  clean="$(head -c 8192 "$1" | LC_ALL=C tr -d '\000' | wc -c)"
  [ "$raw" -eq "$clean" ]
}
```

The same rule explains why `grep -q $'\0'` is meaningless and why a variable set to
binary data arrives as a shorter string than you wrote.

## Process spawns dominate, and they scale with your tree

The measured lesson. A checker that probes every file with a handful of
subprocesses costs a constant number of processes **per file**:

```
per-file probing : 8 processes x 996 files = ~8000 spawns -> 265 s
batched scan     : 2 processes total       ->   1.3 s
```

That is a 200x difference for the same answer, and the slow version looks like a
hang rather than a design problem.

```sh
# slow: a process per file
for f in $(find . -type f); do grep -qU $'\r' "$f" && echo "$f"; done

# fast: one process for the whole tree
find . -type f -print0 | xargs -0 grep -lU $'\r'
```

Then filter the survivors. The expensive predicate (`is_text`) runs on a handful
of candidates instead of on everything.

**`-print0` with `xargs -0`** is the only safe way to pass a file list: it
survives spaces, quotes and newlines in filenames, which word splitting does not.

Watch for flags that cancel each other: `grep -lzU` lists nothing at all, so a
batch scan that silently returns empty is often a flag interaction rather than a
clean tree. Confirm the batch form against a fixture before trusting it.

## Quoting survives exactly as far as the first wrapper

Every layer that re-parses text is a place the escaping changes meaning, and each
one fails differently.

| Layer | Failure |
| --- | --- |
| `sh -c "…"` | inner `$`, backtick and `\` are expanded by the outer shell first |
| `eval "$cmd"` | a second full parse; a value containing code becomes code |
| heredoc `<<EOF` | expands `$`; `<<'EOF'` does not, and is the default you want |
| PowerShell → bash | `\` is not an escape character, so paths and regexes need care |
| a regex substituted into a regex | `\` and `[]` must be escaped twice |

When a value crosses a boundary, stop passing text and start passing bytes or
files. A temporary script is more reliable than an `eval` chain at any depth.

## Do not hold locks across a read-modify-write in a temp file

```sh
# two concurrent runs collide on the same temporary name
tmp="$out.tmp.$$"
```

`$$` is the shell's PID, which is stable across a subshell and can be reused after
one exits. Colliding on a temporary file corrupts whichever run finishes second.
Use `mktemp`, which creates the file exclusively:

```sh
tmp="$(mktemp "${out}.tmp.XXXXXX")" || exit 1
trap 'rm -f "$tmp"' EXIT
```

And note the two-step form `tmp` then `tmp.2` is not a second temp file — it is a
second `mv` racing on the same name. Keep one name.

## `set -e` does not check what you think

`set -e` is disabled for commands on the left of `&&`, `||`, in a condition, and
in a pipeline except the **last** element. So this checks `wc`, not `grep`:

```sh
set -e
grep -q pattern file | wc -l      # green even when grep found nothing
```

Check explicitly where it matters, and never rely on `set -e` as the only
guarantee that something ran.

## Write the result the caller needs

```sh
printf 'normalized %d file(s) (%d scanned)\n' "$changed" "$file_count"
```

The **scanned** count is the part that matters. "Found nothing" is ambiguous
between "looked at 996 files" and "looked at none", and only the second number
tells them apart. A tool that reports what it examined is evidence; one that
reports only findings is an absence of complaints.

## Anti-rationalisations

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "`grep $'\\r'` obviously matches a CR." | It does not. The CR is consumed as the pattern terminator. |
| "It says every file is broken, so it works." | An empty pattern matches everything. Pin the must-not-match case. |
| "sed errored, so it is fine." | The output is empty and the status was discarded. |
| "`GROUPS` is just a variable." | It is a builtin array. The assignment is discarded, silently. |
| "It is a small repo, speed does not matter." | 8 processes per file is 265 s at 1,000 files. It does not scale. |
| "The file list has no spaces." | Until someone adds one. Use `-print0` and `xargs -0`. |
| "The batch scan found nothing, so we are clean." | `grep -lzU` finds nothing regardless. Test the batch form on a fixture. |
| "I read the manual." | Measure it. The manual does not cover the flag interactions. |
